'''Wanten''', [[Operating number|designated]] '''TD-110''' and '''DSS-0956''', was a [[human]] [[Gender|male]] [[First Order officer|officer]] of the [[First Order]] and a former [[stormtrooper]] of the [[Galactic Empire]]. During the [[Galactic Civil War]], TD-110 served on board the [[DS-1 Orbital Battle Station|first Death Star]] in the [[Standard day|days]] prior to the [[Battle of Yavin]]. He was also deployed to the [[Outer Rim Territories|Outer Rim]] [[Planet|world]] of [[Tatooine]] as a [[Stormtrooper Commander|sandtrooper commander]], attempting to reacquire the stolen [[Death Star plans]] from the [[Alliance to Restore the Republic|rebel]] [[droid]]s [[R2-D2]] and [[C-3PO]].

Like other [[Imperial officer]]s and [[soldier]]s, Wanten supported the First Order after the [[Galaxy|Galactic]] Empire [[Galactic Concordance|surrendered]] to the [[New Republic]] and ultimately became a [[commander]] in its [[First Order military|military forces]] in the [[Unknown Regions]]. Stationed on [[Starkiller Base]] during the early [[Standard day|days]] of the [[First Order-Resistance War]], Wanten was [[Death|killed]] by [[J-Squadron]] when the [[Resistance]] [[Battle of Starkiller Base|attacked]] the First Order [[superweapon]].

==Biography==
===Early life===
Wanten<ref name="Escape from Vodran" /> was [[Reproduction|born]] on [[Parsh]]<ref name="Bump" /> between [[20 BBY|20]] to [[13 BBY]],<ref name="Birth" /> where he was part of a [[TD-110's clan|clan]] that eked out a living on the harsh [[Planet|world]]. When the [[Galactic Empire]] arrived on the world, they put the clan to work in mines for them. At some point later, he became a [[stormtrooper]] in the [[Imperial Military]] and was [[Operating number|designated]] TD-110<ref name="Bump" /> and DSS-0956.<ref name="Rise of the Rebellion">{{EncyclopediaCite|The Rise of the Rebellion and the Battle of Yavin}}</ref>

===Hunt for the rebel droids===
{{Quote|These aren't the droids you're looking for.''"<br />"''These aren't the droids we're looking for.''"<br />"''He can go about his business.''"<br />"''You can go about your business.''"<br />"''Move along.''"<br />"''Move along. Move along.|Obi-Wan Kenobi and TD-110|<ref name="Episode IV" />}}
[[File:Mos_Eisley_Stormtroopers.jpg|thumb|left|300px|TD-110 (left) served on Tatooine as a sandtrooper and commander of Foot Patrol 7.]]
In [[1 BBY]],<ref name="Galactic Atlas">''[[Star Wars: Galactic Atlas: Updated Edition]]''</ref> he served as a [[Stormtrooper Commander|stormtrooper commander]]<ref name="Born in the Storm" /> with the rank of [[captain]].<ref name="Odyssey">''[[The Odyssey of Star Wars: An Epic Poem]]''</ref> Commanding [[Foot Patrol 7]] as part of a [[Unidentified sandtrooper regiment|greater regiment]], Wanthen found himself based in [[Mos Eisley]] on [[Tatooine]] after [[Grand Moff]] [[Wilhuff Tarkin]]<ref name="Born in the Storm" /> and the [[Sith Lord]] [[Anakin Skywalker|Darth Vader]] dispatched Foot Patrol 7's detachment<ref name="Rise of the Rebellion" /> to Tatooine for a [[secret mission to Tatooine|mission]] to find two [[Alliance to Restore the Republic]] [[droid]]s.<ref name="Episode IV" /> While on patrol in the [[city]], he and his unit stopped and questioned an [[Obi-Wan Kenobi|old man]], a [[Luke Skywalker|teenager]], a [[C-3PO|protocol droid]], and an [[R2-D2|astromech droid]] on a [[X-34 landspeeder|speeder]] eventually letting the group pass after TD-110 was unknowingly [[mind trick]]ed into doing so. Foot Patrol 7 were later called to reinforce other units by [[Commander]] [[TD-4445]] during a skirmish to prevent the ''[[Millennium Falcon]]'' from leaving one of the city's landing bays, but they did not reach the hangar in time.<ref name="Born in the Storm" />

===Serving aboard the Death Star===
{{Quote|Oh! All this excitement has overrun the circuits of my counterpart here. If you don't mind, I'd like to take him down to maintenance.''"<br />"''All right.|C-3PO and TD-110|<ref name="Episode IV" />}}
[[File:Knock_Head_stormie.png|thumb|right|229px|TD-110 bumped his head on a doorway during his time on the Death Star.]]
Wanten eventually traveled to the [[DS-1 Orbital Battle Station|Death Star]] around the [[time]] [[Princess]] [[Leia Skywalker Organa Solo|Leia Organa]] was [[Rescue of Princess Leia|rescued]]. Wanten had gained an awful [[headache]] as an aftereffect of the mind trick, which meant he had to give [[Commanding officer|command]] of his troops to one of his subordinates. During his time on the Death Star, he realized that the [[droid]]s he encountered in Mos Eisley were the droids the Empire had been looking for, as they contained the [[Death Star plans]].<ref name="Bump" />

On the way to [[Control Room 327]] to control a skirmish there, he hit his head on the doorway and was briefly incapacitated, sparing him the [[death]] that met the other stormtroopers assigned there. Coming to, he noticed the droids that had eluded him and tried to shoot them in an attempt to redeem himself. However, he was stopped by a [[Moff]] who escorted him away for discipline. As a result of his mistakes, he was removed from his post on the Death Star.<ref name="Bump" /> This allowed him to avoid its destruction in the [[Battle of Yavin]] that took the lives of his comrades.<ref name="Episode IV" /> However, this left Wanten on Tatooine, who would later be disappointed about having missed the "important" battles, such as [[Battle of Endor|Endor]] and [[Battle of Jakku|Jakku]].<ref name="Escape from Vodran" />

===First Order service===
Decades later, during the [[Cold War|conflict]] between the [[First Order]] and the [[Resistance]], Wanten<ref name="Escape from Vodran" /><ref name="NYCC">{{SW|url=news/nycc-2017-highlights-from-a-certain-point-of-view-author-panel|text=NYCC 2017: Highlights from the ''From a Certain Point of View'' Author Panel}}</ref> served as a commander in the [[First Order military]] and was stationed on [[Vodran]], where he hoped to eventually be promoted to the [[Bittelari Cluster]].<ref name="Escape from Vodran" /> When the Resistance [[Battle of Starkiller Base|destroyed]] [[Starkiller Base]], Wanten was rammed by a [[Griffin-class light shuttle|''Griffin''-class light shuttle]] by [[J-Squadron]] which killed him.<ref name="Attack on Starkiller Base" />

==Personality and traits==
{{Quote|The Force can have a strong influence on the weak-minded.|Obi-Wan Kenobi|audio=MindTrick.flac|<ref name="Episode IV" />}}
A [[human]] [[Gender|male]], Wanten had a thick trunk, beady [[Color|blue]] [[eye]]s, and a [[Corellian]] [[nose]] that made it difficult to wear a stormtrooper helmet comfortably. By [[34 ABY]], he was bald, and his once-athletic body had turned fat.<ref name="Escape from Vodran" />

Wanten was weak-willed, and therefore, easily susceptible to the influence of [[the Force]].<ref name="Episode IV" /> According to his subordinate [[Sardis Ramsin]], he often said everything twice.<ref name="Born in the Storm" />

==Behind the scenes==
Wanten is a combination of multiple stormtrooper characters, and as such, multiple actors. The individual who played the mind-tricked [[Desert stormtrooper|sandtrooper]] on Tatooine was [[Anthony Forrest]], who also portrayed the character [[Laze Loneozner]] in a scene cut from the same film, ''A New Hope''.<ref name="CC 20193">{{SW|url=news/billy-dee-williams-alan-tudyk-star-wars-celebration-chicago|text=Billy Dee Williams, Alan Tudyk Announced for ''Star Wars'' Celebration Chicago}}</ref> For a great deal of time, Forrest's role as the stormtrooper remained unknown to the ''[[Star Wars]]'' fandom until an image of him on set in costume, albeit with the helmet off, surfaced.<ref name="Let Me See">{{InsiderCite|216|Let Me See Your Identification}}</ref> The helmet he wore in this scene was sold at auction in [[2024]], the listing for which revealed that "Harding" was etched into the helmet's interior, assumed to refer to stuntman [[Reg Harding]].<ref name="Propstore">{{WebCite|url=https://propstoreauction.com/lot-details/index/catalog/399/lot/145214/363-Screen-matched-Imperial-Stormtrooper-Sandtrooper-Helmet-from-Jedi-Mind-Trick-Scene-Move-Along-Move-Along-STAR-WARS-A-NEW-HOP|text=Lot # 363: STAR WARS: A NEW HOPE (1977) - Screen-matched Imperial Stormtrooper (Sandtrooper) Helmet from Jedi Mind Trick Scene ( Move Along, Move Along )|archivedate=20250620135842}}</ref> This may imply that the role was recast from Harding to Forrest, or that the helmet was used in another scene where it was worn by Harding.

The head-bumping trooper was played by an uncredited actor in the [[1977]] film [[Star Wars: Episode IV A New Hope|''Star Wars'': Episode IV ''A New Hope'']].<ref name="Episode IV" /> While entering his scene, the actor playing the stormtrooper inadvertently bumped his head on a door frame. Wanten was later identified in the short stories "[[Born in the Storm]]" and "[[Bump]]" from the [[2017]] anthology book ''[[From a Certain Point of View]]''<ref name="Born in the Storm" /><ref name="Bump" /> as well as the novel ''[[Join the Resistance: Escape from Vodran]]'', both of which were released on the same day.<ref name="Escape from Vodran" />

[[Laurie Goode]] once recalled bumping his head on set while playing a stormtrooper, leading many fans to believe that he may have portrayed the trooper.<ref name="Goode Interview">{{Blogspot|subdomain=starwarsinterviews1|url=2010/05/laurie-goode-interview-saurin.html|name=Star Wars Interviews|text=Laurie Goode interview}}</ref> [[Michael Leader]] recalled a similar event, and when the actor died in [[2016]], at least one media obituary identified him as the actor.<ref name="Media Obituary">{{WebCite|url=https://www.cnet.com/news/stormtrooper-who-bonked-head-in-original-star-wars-movie-has-died|text=Stormtrooper who bonked head in original Star Wars movie has died|author=Fashingbauer Cooper, Gael|date=2016-08-26|work=CNet|archivedate=20200911125425}}</ref>

In the [[2002]] film [[Star Wars: Episode II Attack of the Clones|''Star Wars'': Episode II ''Attack of the Clones'']], director [[George Lucas]] paid homage to the "head-bumping stormtrooper" bump scene by having [[Jango Fett]] bump his head on the door of the ''[[Slave I]]''.<ref name="AOTC">{{Film|II}}</ref> In the [[2004]] DVD re-release of the ''[[Star Wars]]'' [[original trilogy]], the scene was enhanced with a sound effect to draw more attention to it.<ref name="2004 Re-Release of OT">[[Star Wars Trilogy (DVD)|''Star Wars Trilogy'' DVD]]</ref> In the DVD commentary of ''Attack of the Clones'', Lucas suggested that Jango Fett's "bumping-head trait" was cloned into all the stormtroopers, implying that Wanten was a clone.<ref name="DVD Commentary AOTC">{{Film|II}} DVD commentary</ref> However, this was contradicted by Wanten's backstory in ''From a Certain Point of View''.<ref name="Bump" />

